/// A named argument narrows the overload candidates before types are weighed.
///
/// Only candidates having a parameter of that name remain.  Names are syntax,
/// so the narrowing is available whether or not any argument's type is known --
/// which is what lets a compiler do it early and an interpreter reach the same
/// answer late.
///
/// ⚠️ Read which line earns the feature.  Adjust(1) and Adjust(Index => 1)
/// agree, because an exact Integer match already beats a widening to Single;
/// naming the parameter documents the choice without changing it.  It is
/// Adjust(Percentage => 1) that positional calling cannot express AT ALL -- with
/// an Integer argument there is no way to reach the Single overload otherwise.
///
/// ⚠️ Parameter names are NOT part of a signature.  Two functions differing only
/// in parameter names are one signature and a duplicate declaration; were they
/// distinct, every positional call would be ambiguous between them and a
/// feature meant to remove doubt would create it.
///
/// Fails with: Expect expression!  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
/// Parsing fails at '=>' before the Resolver can object to the duplicate name,
/// so this reports a syntax error rather than ''Adjust'' is already defined.'
///
function Adjust (Index : Integer) : String;
begin
    Exit 'by index';
end

function Adjust (Percentage : Single) : String;
begin
    Exit 'by percent';
end

test 'A named argument selects among overloads';
begin
    AssertEqual ('by index',   Adjust (1));
    AssertEqual ('by index',   Adjust (Index => 1));
    AssertEqual ('by percent', Adjust (Percentage => 1));
end
